<section class="hero hero--page">
  <div class="wrap wrap--narrow">
    <p class="eyebrow">Device guide</p>
    <h1>Which devices can switch?</h1>
    <p class="lead">
      Bluetooth devices handle "more than one computer" in a few different ways, and an app can only
      move some of them. Here's how to tell which kind you have, and what to do with each.
      AutoSwitch shows the kind for you when you add a device.
    </p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="section section--tint">
  <div class="wrap">
    <ul class="kinds">
      <li class="kind kind--yes">
        <p class="kind__verdict">Switches automatically</p>
        <h2>Hands off by pairing</h2>
        <p>
          The device holds one pairing. As soon as one computer lets go, the next can pair, without
          you touching the device. AutoSwitch unpairs it when the KVM leaves and pairs it when the
          KVM arrives (<strong>Manage pairing</strong> on).
        </p>
        <p class="kind__examples"><strong>Examples:</strong> Apple Magic Trackpad, Magic Keyboard, Magic Mouse.</p>
        <p class="kind__platforms">Mac ✓ · Windows ✓</p>
      </li>
      <li class="kind kind--no">
        <p class="kind__verdict">Can't be switched by an app</p>
        <h2>Picks the computer itself</h2>
        <p>
          The device remembers several computers, one per slot or profile, and a key on the device
          chooses which one it talks to. No computer can change that slot. Removing the pairing on
          one computer breaks its slot, and you'd have to pair it again from the device.
        </p>
        <p class="kind__examples"><strong>Examples:</strong> ZMK and QMK wireless keyboards. (Most Logitech Easy-Switch devices can be switched: see the next card.)</p>
        <p class="kind__tip"><strong>What to do:</strong> use the device's own switch key. Or plug it into the KVM with a USB cable: many of these keyboards prefer USB when it's connected, and the KVM switches it like any wired keyboard. Leave <strong>Manage pairing</strong> off.</p>
      </li>
      <li class="kind kind--yes">
        <p class="kind__verdict">Switches automatically</p>
        <h2>Switches channel on request</h2>
        <p>
          Like the previous kind, it remembers a computer per channel, but the computer it's
          connected to can tell it to change channel. When the KVM leaves, AutoSwitch sends the
          device to the other computer's channel. Its pairings stay exactly as they are.
        </p>
        <p class="kind__examples"><strong>Examples:</strong> Logitech Easy-Switch keyboards and mice, like the K380 (they support Logitech's "Change Host" command). AutoSwitch checks the device to be sure.</p>
        <p class="kind__platforms">Mac ✓ · Windows ✓</p>
      </li>
      <li class="kind kind--no">
        <p class="kind__verdict">Can't be switched by an app</p>
        <h2>Re-pairs by hand</h2>
        <p>
          The device remembers one Bluetooth computer. To move it, you press its pairing button and
          pair it again. After a computer removes it, it still points at that computer, and the other
          one can't pair until you press the button.
        </p>
        <p class="kind__examples"><strong>Examples:</strong> Xbox, PlayStation and Nintendo controllers.</p>
        <p class="kind__tip"><strong>What to do:</strong> keep it on the computer you play on, or connect it with a cable through the KVM. Or pair it to a <strong>Bluetooth-to-USB adapter</strong> plugged into the KVM (for controllers, e.g. the 8BitDo USB Wireless Adapter 2, which works on Windows and Mac): then the KVM switches it like a wired controller. Leave <strong>Manage pairing</strong> off.</p>
      </li>
      <li class="kind kind--partly">
        <p class="kind__verdict">Mac yes, Windows not yet</p>
        <h2>Joins whichever computer connects</h2>
        <p>
          The device remembers several computers and goes to whichever one connects to it. The Mac
          app disconnects it when the KVM leaves and connects it when the KVM arrives
          (<strong>Manage pairing</strong> off). Windows has no way to disconnect a Bluetooth device
          without unpairing it, so the Windows app can't do this yet.
        </p>
        <p class="kind__examples"><strong>Examples:</strong> many headphones and speakers.</p>
        <p class="kind__platforms">Mac: supported, being tested · Windows: not yet</p>
      </li>
    </ul>
    <div class="receiver-tip">
      <h2>Make a Bluetooth device "wired" with an adapter</h2>
      <p>
        For a device AutoSwitch can't switch, a small Bluetooth-to-USB adapter plugged into the KVM
        does the job: the device pairs once with the adapter, and the KVM switches the adapter like
        any USB device. For game controllers, the 8BitDo USB Wireless Adapter 2 pairs Xbox,
        PlayStation and Switch Pro controllers (Bluetooth ones only). For keyboards and mice there
        are do-it-yourself bridges such as HID Remapper's Bluetooth build or a Raspberry Pi Pico W
        keyboard bridge. They don't carry a Magic Trackpad's gestures, so keep AutoSwitch for that.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div class="receiver-tip">
      <h2>Using a USB receiver?</h2>
      <p>
        Logitech Unifying, Logi Bolt and Lightspeed receivers are faster than Bluetooth, and they
        don't need AutoSwitch at all: plug the receiver into your KVM's USB hub, and the KVM switches
        it like a wired keyboard. Use the KVM's plain USB port, not a "keyboard/mouse emulation"
        port, which often doesn't pass receivers through properly.
      </p>
    </div>
    <p class="kinds__note">
      For the kinds it can't switch, AutoSwitch still shows whether the device is connected.
    </p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="section">
  <div class="wrap wrap--narrow">
    <p class="eyebrow">Not sure?</p>
    <h2>Test your device in a minute</h2>
    <ol class="steps steps--list">
      <li>
        <span class="steps__badge">1</span>
        <h3>Connect it to one computer</h3>
        <p>Make sure it works there as usual.</p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <span class="steps__badge">2</span>
        <h3>Remove it there</h3>
        <p>In that computer's Bluetooth settings, remove (forget) the device.</p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <span class="steps__badge">3</span>
        <h3>Pair it on the other computer</h3>
        <p>
          Without pressing anything on the device. If it pairs, it <strong>hands off by pairing</strong>
          and AutoSwitch can switch it. If it only pairs after you press a button on it, it
          <strong>re-pairs by hand</strong>. If it has a switch key for computers, it
          <strong>picks the computer itself</strong>.
        </p>
      </li>
    </ol>
    <p>
      AutoSwitch guesses the kind from the device's maker and type, and says so: <em>likely</em> or
      <em>probably</em> when it isn't a known model. Found a device it gets wrong?
      <a href="{{ site.issues }}">Tell us</a> and it'll be recognized in a future version.
    </p>
    <p><a class="btn btn--primary" href="/#download">Get AutoSwitch KVM</a></p>
  </div>
</section>
